WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.023 --> 00:00:04.752
Napoleon Bonaparte believed that destiny had chosen him for greatness.

2
00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:10.676
A dictator, military genius, and social reformer, he inspired both awe and hatred.

3
00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:17.168
His ambition brought him amazing military and political success, yet his tactics were often ruthless.

4
00:00:17.169 --> 00:00:23.679
Nearly two hundred years after his death, scholars still debate Napoleon’s motives and the value of his methods.

5
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:32.373
He remains a controversial figure because despite his historic achievements, he ended his days in exile, branded an "enemy of humanity."

6
00:00:32.374 --> 00:00:39.465
Napoleon Bonaparte was born on August 15, 1769, on the Mediterranean island of Corsica.

7
00:00:39.466 --> 00:00:45.862
The rugged island fought unsuccessfully for independence first from Italy and then from France.

8
00:00:45.863 --> 00:00:52.408
Napoleon’s parents supported the Corsican struggle, teaching their eight children the value of independence.

9
00:00:52.409 --> 00:00:56.346
Although descended from nobility, the family was not rich.

10
00:00:56.348 --> 00:01:02.567
They used political connections to get Napoleon accepted into a French military academy at the age of nine.

11
00:01:02.568 --> 00:01:10.456
There, his thick Corsican accent and broken French made him an outcast among his classmates, many of whom were wealthy nobility.

12
00:01:10.457 --> 00:01:21.007
Napoleon was a dedicated student, however, and because of his excellent academic record, he graduated at the age of 16, joining the Royal Artillery as a junior officer.

13
00:01:21.008 --> 00:01:26.244
Napoleon began his military career as an artillery commander in the south of France.

14
00:01:26.245 --> 00:01:34.443
He spent his wages on books, instead of wine and women, and impressed senior officers, who noticed his intelligence and discipline.

15
00:01:34.444 --> 00:01:42.333
In 1789, the French Revolution began&#8212;commoners were rising up against the monarchy that had ruled France for centuries.

16
00:01:42.334 --> 00:01:49.990
Napoleon embraced the ideals of the revolution, which aimed to end economic and social divides between the nobility and the commoners.

17
00:01:49.991 --> 00:01:53.162
But he also saw a chance to advance his career.

18
00:01:53.163 --> 00:02:03.203
In 1793, the 24-year-old Napoleon was promoted to major and sent to Toulon, a French town that British troops had invaded in hopes of ending the revolution.

19
00:02:03.204 --> 00:02:09.194
When Napoleon drove the British out, the victory ignited his ambition and fueled his sense of destiny.

20
00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:14.191
Word of Napoleon’s success reached Paris, and he rose quickly in the military.

21
00:02:14.192 --> 00:02:25.406
When rebels who wanted to restore the monarchy planned to take over the republic in October 1795, Napoleon was put in charge of defending the Tuileries Palace, the offices of the government.

22
00:02:25.407 --> 00:02:31.668
His forces placed cannons in the streets around the palace and met the rebels with brutal cannon fire.

23
00:02:31.669 --> 00:02:36.664
After only 15 minutes, hundreds of rebels were dead and the others had fled.

24
00:02:36.665 --> 00:02:44.124
Napoleon, just 26 years old, became a national hero, celebrated for crushing the uprising with force and speed.

25
00:02:44.125 --> 00:02:54.659
He was soon made commander of the French army and led his troops against Italy and Austria, where his use of artillery and deception created tactical advantages that crippled the enemy.

26
00:02:54.660 --> 00:03:03.653
He was now a living legend&#8212;a general who slept little, worked constantly, and captured the love of his soldiers by leading the way into battle himself.

27
00:03:03.654 --> 00:03:09.963
In 1799, Napoleon returned from a military campaign in Egypt to a France in turmoil.

28
00:03:09.964 --> 00:03:14.409
The republic was running out of money and being threatened by foreign powers.

29
00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:23.357
The population was angry with the new government, and some politicians believed that the military must be put in power to prevent the return of the monarchy.

30
00:03:23.358 --> 00:03:26.686
Napoleon was asked to help overthrow the government.

31
00:03:26.687 --> 00:03:31.459
Sensing an opportunity to increase his power, he agreed to participate in a coup.

32
00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:38.061
A new government was created, and Napoleon seized control, becoming head of the republic, or "first consul."

33
00:03:38.062 --> 00:03:43.561
Acting as dictator of France, he conducted himself like a soldier even in politics.

34
00:03:43.562 --> 00:03:53.321
He ruled the country through censorship and surveillance, shutting down many Paris newspapers and using secret police to spy on citizens from all walks of life.

35
00:03:53.322 --> 00:04:01.615
People who opposed him faced prison or death. Napoleon, now thirty years old, held France in an iron grip.

36
00:04:01.616 --> 00:04:11.641
Despite Napoleon’s harsh tactics, his glory as a military genius and hero of the revolution had not been forgotten, and many people in France supported him.

37
00:04:11.642 --> 00:04:17.361
Their faith seemed to be rewarded when he began a program of social and governmental reform.

38
00:04:17.362 --> 00:04:25.854
Napoleon made sweeping changes to the French banking, judicial, and educational systems, ensuring equality for all French citizens.

39
00:04:25.855 --> 00:04:32.012
The civil laws that were created at his direction are known as the Napoleonic Code and remain in use today.

40
00:04:32.013 --> 00:04:41.133
As commander of what was then the largest army in Europe, Napoleon also gave soldiers the right to rise in rank by merit rather than social class.

41
00:04:41.134 --> 00:04:48.847
Acclaimed by a grateful nation, Napoleon drew upon his sense of destiny and named himself emperor in 1804.

42
00:04:48.848 --> 00:04:52.996
As emperor, Napoleon aimed to expand France’s boundaries.

43
00:04:52.997 --> 00:04:59.176
Ambitious to control all of Europe, he achieved his greatest military victory in the Battle of Austerlitz.

44
00:04:59.177 --> 00:05:07.550
Fighting against Austrian and Russian troops, he displayed tactical brilliance by tricking the enemy into thinking he had fewer troops than he did.

45
00:05:07.551 --> 00:05:13.666
The French lost 9,000 men, while Russia and Austria together lost 25,000.

46
00:05:13.667 --> 00:05:24.027
Napoleon insisted that destiny would always lead him to victory, and over the next years, he fought in Prussia, Poland, and Russia, claiming new territories for France.

47
00:05:24.028 --> 00:05:29.598
But by 1812, the tide was turning against him and his popularity was falling.

48
00:05:29.599 --> 00:05:45.462
Wars in Spain and Portugal were going badly, and in the fall and winter of 1812, Napoleon lost more than half a million of his soldiers while retreating from an ill-fated campaign in Russia; they starved, froze, deserted, or were captured by enemy forces.

49
00:05:45.463 --> 00:05:52.971
By 1814, Austria, Prussia, Russia, and Great Britain had allied to attack France and overthrow Napoleon.

50
00:05:52.972 --> 00:05:58.787
When Paris was captured in April 1814, Napoleon abdicated the throne.

51
00:05:58.788 --> 00:06:02.835
Only Napoleon’s fame and power kept him from being executed.

52
00:06:02.836 --> 00:06:07.288
He was banished to the Italian island of Elba and guarded day and night.

53
00:06:07.289 --> 00:06:16.218
Letters and newspapers enabled him to follow events in France, however, and when word came that the monarchy would be restored, he escaped from the island.

54
00:06:16.219 --> 00:06:22.988
He returned to France in 1815, arriving in Paris on March 20 in the company of a loyal army.

55
00:06:22.989 --> 00:06:31.765
Napoleon was welcomed back by the French people and reclaimed the title of emperor, but the countries allied against him began to gather troops for an attack.

56
00:06:31.766 --> 00:06:39.139
He faced them in June 1815 in the Battle of Waterloo, which resulted in a crushing defeat for Napoleon and his army.

57
00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:44.597
One hundred days after his triumphant return to France, he was forced to abdicate again.

58
00:06:44.598 --> 00:06:50.903
He was banished from France and sent to St. Helena, a remote and inescapable island in the Atlantic Ocean.

59
00:06:50.904 --> 00:06:56.006
After six years in exile, Napoleon died on May 5, 1821.

60
00:06:56.007 --> 00:07:02.178
More books have been written about Napoleon Bonaparte than about any other figure in recent history.

61
00:07:02.179 --> 00:07:06.897
Scholars are still trying to understand whether he was a tyrant or a visionary.

62
00:07:06.898 --> 00:07:12.960
Millions of people died on his battlefields, and millions of people benefited from his reforms.

63
00:07:12.961 --> 00:07:20.811
Many European nations still use the Napoleonic Code, and his military tactics are studied by armed forces around the world.

64
00:07:20.812 --> 00:07:29.136
Napoleon’s belief in destiny drove him to greatness, but his hunger for power may have destroyed his chance to achieve lasting glory.

